//
// A BOUNDED TEMPLATE TAKES THE CALL ITS BOUND ADMITS (`@FR-D-Rank`, `@FR-G-Sat`, `@FR-Disp-Select`).
// A template member of an overload set is a candidate only where its variables bind AND the
// bound types satisfy its bounds — structurally, by the methods visible for the type.  So in
// a set of a concrete member, a bounded template and a bare one: the concrete member takes
// its own type, the bounded template takes a type that has the interface's method, and the
// bare template takes a type that has none (its bound fails, so it is simply no candidate).
//
// Ranks hand-computed:
//   rh(RB) concrete EXACT                         → x.n + 100     = 102
//   rh(RA) RA has `tag`: the bounded one, `$0:RTagged` against `$0` by bound set → tag + 1 = 41
//   rh(RC) RC has no `tag`: the bounded one is no candidate, the bare one → 3
//   rh(5)  integer has no `tag`                   → 3
//
// Axes it MOVES: which member takes the call (concrete, bounded, bare), whether the type has
//   the bound's method, a record and a scalar argument.
// Held FIXED: one bound, a plain method member, integer results.
//
// @falsified-at: none — a conformance guard; every cell answered as hand-computed on 1e0f8d2ed,
//   interpret and native.  It is what the @PLN91 elaborator's `satisfies` port is gated on.
// CHANNEL: the assert.
// WITNESS: the message prints the four answers.
// HOLDS: a type with the method still reaches the bounded member past the bare one.

interface RTagged { fn tag(self: Self) -> integer }
struct RA { n: integer }
struct RB { n: integer }
struct RC { n: integer }
fn tag(self: RA) -> integer { self.n * 10 }

fn rh(x: RB) -> integer { x.n + 100 }
fn rh<T: RTagged>(x: T) -> integer { x.tag() + 1 }
fn rh<T>(_x: T) -> integer { 3 }

fn main() {
  a = rh(RA { n: 4 });
  b = rh(RB { n: 2 });
  c = rh(RC { n: 9 });
  d = rh(5);
  assert(a == 41 && b == 102 && c == 3 && d == 3, "bounded selection: {a} {b} {c} {d}");
}
